{
  "table_id": "Table_S20",
  "display_number": "S20",
  "title": "Supplementary Table S20. Sensitivity of four-group exposure-pattern associations to alternative classification thresholds.",
  "panel": "",
  "panel_title": "",
  "notes_before": [
    "Adjusted BAA differences (years) from unweighted linear mixed-effects models (maximum likelihood) with participant-specific random intercepts, adjusted for the main covariate set (N = 6,471 observations); estimates were replicated on the final analytical dataset. The Original K-means rows use the same four exposure groups as the descriptive joint-exposure analysis but are fitted without inverse-probability weights so that all classification schemes share a common specification; the IPW-weighted joint-exposure estimates are reported in Table S16. The high-EVI/high-O₃ versus reference estimates and their confidence intervals are compared across classification rules. A non-significant contrast does not establish equivalence or robustness."
  ],
  "notes_after": [
    "Across alternative classification thresholds, the high-EVI/high-O₃ group was not statistically distinguishable from the reference in the reported comparisons. These results describe sensitivity of exposure-pattern associations. Product-term analyses did not provide precise evidence of departure from additivity of mean BAA differences."
  ],
